 "productionNotes": "No source consulted gives a production total for the half-ton C10 square body, and the figures that circulate cannot all be true. Hagerty says Chevrolet sold 666,619 C/K trucks for the 1973 model year and notes that GMC figures were not available. Drivingline says more than a million examples were sold in the first year. J. Kelly Flory's reference book American Light Trucks and Utility Vehicles, 1967-1989, read as Google Books snippets, puts all 1973 model year Chevrolet light-truck production at 897,098, which covers El Camino, vans and the LUV as well as C/K pickups, so Drivingline's million can only be right if it counts GMC, Blazer and Suburban too. For 1978 Flory gives 1,233,712 Chevrolet light trucks of every type, while a classic.com listing page says 1,317,466 C/K trucks were sold that year, a figure larger than Chevrolet's entire light-truck output unless it includes GMC or counts calendar-year sales. Classic Decoder's over 75,000 by 1975 and High Flow Fuel's estimate of over 10 million across the run fit none of these. For 1987 Flory gives 1,072,530 Chevrolet light trucks of all types. No source breaks out the C10 alone, regular cab against crew cab, Fleetside against Stepside or two-wheel against four-wheel drive, so this page prints no total and no variant counts. Price is a similar gap: Flory gives a 1973 base price range of $2,107 to $4,102 for all Chevrolet light trucks and a Chevrolet average base price of $3,195, and an average of $10,728 for 1987, but there is no documented US list price for a specific C10 configuration in the sources consulted. Nothing here came from the GM archive or a registry; GM's own 1987 dealer kit was read for specifications only.",
